"""The Silent's strategy, shared by planning and individual decisions.

Owner's guidance: consistent Weak and Block, while taking damage scaling for the bosses.

The numbers in the text are what the highest-rated Spire Codex players did with the Silent on the
pinned game version: 166 solo A10 standard runs of builds v0.107.1 and v0.107.0 by the 12 highest-rated
accounts that have them, 142 won, read 2026-11-25 with ``python -m sts_agent.pilot players --character
SILENT --words Shiv,Poison`` (the decks carried into each act boss and what was taken at 3058 card
rewards, counted by ``evidence.deck_balance``'s role rules). The build names come from the Spire Codex
guides (builds guide and Silent tier list), which describe later patches.
"""

PROMPT_VERSION = "silent-strategic-planner-prompt-5"

DECK_GUIDANCE = """\
THE SILENT'S DECK. Stability comes first: Weak on the enemy every turn, and Block that does not hang on one card. What that deck looks like is known. In 166 solo A10 runs on this game version by the highest-rated Spire Codex players (142 won), the deck entering the act 1, 2 and 3 bosses held about 22, 29 and 37 cards. It is not kept thin: they took 86% of the card rewards in act 1, 72% in act 2 and 60% in act 3, as often with a large deck as with a small one, and what they added after act 1 was Skills and Powers (attacks stay near 8 while Skills go from 11 to 21).

What they took when it was offered: Block that grows (Dexterity, a Power that gains Block every turn or every card) four times in five when the deck had none and two times in three after; a damage-scaling Power four times in ten as the first or second and three in ten as the third or fourth; a card that makes or pays for Shivs, a Block card, a card that draws or discards, and other utility about one time in three whatever the deck already held. What they left: an attack that only deals damage (one in six in act 1, one in twenty after) and Poison cards (one in six in act 1, one in fifteen after). So take Block scaling on sight, keep adding Block, draw and utility in every act, and stop adding plain attacks early: deck_balance marks damage over-supplied once the deck holds more damage cards than those decks did (from the fifth in act 1, the seventh in act 2, the ninth after) and never marks Block over-supplied. In Act 1 still secure enough immediate damage to end hallway fights before chip damage adds up.

The deck_balance track "weak" counts the cards that apply Weak or lower enemy Strength (the starter's Neutralize included): two, and three once the deck passes 30 cards, which is what those decks held. While it is short a Weak source comes first, and one that also blocks or deals damage is better than a card that only applies Weak; two more than that is too many. Removals: three to four a run, half of them in act 2; starter Strikes go first (three in four removals in act 1, half later) and Defends stay. Count what a draw card can find, what energy remains to play it, and whether a discard outlet and its Sly payoff can be drawn and used together; treat Sly as a payoff for explicit discard during the turn.

Damage scaling is taken for the bosses: those decks held one source at the act 1 boss, two or three at the act 2 boss and three or four at the act 3 boss; plan the next one before each boss, not after. Shivs are the route on this game version: generators, then the Powers and attacks that pay for every Shiv or every card played; check the boss for limits or punishment on many card plays. Poison is the alternative only when the cards already held give repeatable application and a payoff; check Artifact, several targets and how soon the damage is needed. The community guides also rate a discard engine (cards that draw and discard feeding Sly cards, with relics that pay per discard); take its pieces for the draw they give and count the engine only when the relic or the Sly payoff is held. A single generator, payoff or Poison card is evidence of a possible plan; check the rest of the deck before counting a boss need as covered, and do not open a second route while the first still lacks its payoff."""

ELITE_GUIDANCE = """\
THE SILENT'S ELITE ROUTES. The Silent is vulnerable early in Act 1 and takes elites selectively in every act. Two measurements bound the judgment. In simulated A10 runs a strong drafter lost about one elite in seven in act 1 and one in six in act 2 with the Silent, against one in twenty-five and one in eleven with the Ironclad, and an elite it beat cost about a third of max HP. The highest-rated players take them all the same: 2.4 elites in act 1, 2.2 in act 2 and 1.8 in act 3 a run, losing 15%, 19% and 12% of max HP to each, and they enter the act 1 boss at half HP and the act 2 boss at 60%. What their deck holds by the act 1 boss is about ten added cards: four that deal damage (Shiv generators among them), two or three Block cards, one damage-scaling Power, a Block-scaling Power in half the runs, and two or three draw or utility cards. Read an elite as affordable when the deck is on that pace, can answer the elite's likely big turns and deal enough damage before it scales, and the route has preparation or recovery; compare a safer route with fewer or later elites against the relic upside when it is not. Act 1 can plan zero elites when the deck, HP, or recovery cannot support one. Weak and Block can make the margin safer when the enemy and timing allow it. Use observed elite outcomes and the worst projected stretches before a rest site to judge whether a later elite is affordable. An unresolved gap can justify skipping an elite even at high HP. State which elite, if any, is acceptable and what would change that decision."""
